    metaDesc:'Nepal concentrates almost all its A-Level teaching in one valley. What that has cost families elsewhere, and what live delivery changes.',
    intro:'Nepal has a schooling geography I have not seen replicated anywhere else in ninety-nine markets. Almost the entire A-Level sector — the leading schools, the A-Level colleges, the international provision — sits inside a single valley. For a family in Pokhara, Biratnagar, Bharatpur or Butwal, choosing that route at grade 11 has meant one thing: sending a sixteen-year-old to Kathmandu, and paying for a second household to do it.',
    sections:[
      {h:'What that has actually cost families',p:'A second rent, a separated household, a child living away from home at sixteen, and often a parent splitting time between two cities. I want to be careful not to romanticise or criticise — families have made that choice deliberately and it has worked for a great many students. But it is worth naming the cost plainly, because it is substantial and because it has been the only option rather than a preference. When the alternative to a subject teacher is a second household, something has gone wrong with the distribution rather than the education.'},
      {h:'What a live class changes',p:'Four students in Pokhara, Biratnagar and Bharatpur, plus two in Nairobi and one in the Gulf, is a full A-Level class taught by a subject specialist. The child stays home. The family keeps one household. The examination board is the same one the Kathmandu colleges use, the qualification is identical, and the student sits it at authorised provision with travel a few times a year rather than a permanent move. That is the whole proposition in Nepal, and it is the clearest geographic argument in our coverage after the Maldives.'},
      {h:'And inside the valley too',p:'Kathmandu families are not exempt from the other arithmetic. A good A-Level college still cannot run Further Mathematics for four students, or a third science, or Computer Science for two. Admission to the strongest institutions is genuinely competitive, and a student arriving mid-year or changing college has limited options. We have no waiting list and a child starts within a week of the assessment.'},
      {h:'The households already connected internationally',p:'A great many Nepali families have a parent or older sibling working abroad — the Gulf, Malaysia, Korea, Japan, India — and remittance income funds a considerable share of private education spending here. I want to put that respectfully, because it is a source of pride in most households rather than a difficulty: families are investing in routes their own generation did not have. The practical point is that a qualification read directly by universities abroad is worth more to a household already connected internationally than to one that is not. UCAS reads A-Levels natively; Australian, Japanese, Indian, Canadian and American universities read them and the IB directly, with no equivalence step in any of them.'},
      {h:'The clock, which is genuinely unique',p:'Nepal runs UTC+5:45 — the only forty-five-minute offset in the world — and I mention the forty-five minutes rather than rounding because getting it wrong is a small tell that a provider has not paid attention. Against our UTC+3 that is two hours and forty-five minutes ahead: a four o\u2019clock class in Kathmandu is quarter past one in the afternoon for our teachers, six is quarter past three. After-school works, and it works identically in Butwal or Biratnagar.'},
      {h:'What I will not claim',p:'That Nepali schooling needs improving — the A-Level colleges in the valley are good and do not need my opinion. That we teach the SEE or the NEB examinations — we do not, and we are not a registered Nepali school. And that I know Nepal\u2019s home-education position — we could not verify it and say so. What we offer is one thing done properly: the subject specialist, delivered where the family already lives.'},
    ],
    faqs:[
      {q:'Would our child still need to move to Kathmandu at grade 11?',a:'Not for the teaching. Live delivery supplies A-Level subject specialists in Pokhara, Biratnagar, Bharatpur and Butwal identically, with examination travel a few times a year rather than a permanent move.'},
      {q:'We are already at an A-Level college in the valley — what would we gain?',a:'Usually a subject it cannot run for four students. If your college covers what your child needs, we will tell you so.'},
      {q:'What time are classes?',a:'After-school. Nepal is two hours and forty-five minutes ahead of our teaching base, so a four o\u2019clock class in Kathmandu is quarter past one in the afternoon for our teachers.'},
      {q:'Which universities read A-Levels directly?',a:'UCAS reads them natively, and Australian, Japanese, Indian, Canadian and American universities read A-Levels and the IB directly, with no equivalence step.'},
    ],
    conclusion:'A whole country\u2019s A-Level teaching in one valley, and a class that reaches the rest of it. Book an assessment for your child, or start with our Pokhara and Biratnagar pages.',
